<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Policoro</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Policoro is a town in the province of Matera in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinni">Sinni</a> and Agri rivers. It stands on the site of the ancient Greek city of Heraclea, founded in 433 BCE, and some scholars link its hill with the earlier Ionian colony of Siris. The town's National Museum of the Siritide displays finds from both cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/policoro.jpg" alt="Policoro" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Antonelli · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Policoro,_piazza_Eraclea_-_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Policoro is a comune on the Ionian coast, about 70 km from Taranto and 60 km from Matera. It is bounded by the towns of Rotondella, Scanzano Jonico and Tursi. The climate is hot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, with mild winters and warm to hot, dry summers; snowfall is so rare that the townspeople remember it as a special event. The population swells in summer with tourists who come to the Lido di Policoro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The ruins of the ancient Heraclea lie within the modern comune. The city was founded by colonists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a>, present-day Taranto, and Thurii, and in very little time became richer and better known than the nearby town of Siris. In 280 BCE the Battle of Heraclea was fought there during the war between Taranto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tables-of-heraclea">Tables of Heraclea</a> date to this period; they are bronze tablets with texts in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> on the public and constitutional rules and laws of the town, among them the Lex Iulia Municipalis, and are now in the Archeology Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><p>In the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> the whole area depopulated and Heraclea lost its prestige, becoming a small village on a hill. The time and circumstances of its final extinction are wholly unknown. The site was desolate, and the neighbouring district, once regarded as one of the most fertile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was still almost uninhabited in the mid-19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The present comune arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, in the 11th and 12th centuries, as a feudal estate and then a village. It developed around a baronial castle, used as a residence from about 1000 for hunting in the vast forests owned by local noble families such as the Berlingieri, and sheltered a population from nearby villages that were threatened by invasions and raids. Only after 1950, when the marshy and unhealthy land had been reclaimed and an agrarian reform had split and redistributed the land, did Policoro receive municipal autonomy. Families moved in from all parts of the region, producing a large demographic surge, and the town now has some 18,000 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Policoro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Heraclea was built on a plateau near the Siris and Aciris rivers, at modern <b>Policoro</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/heraclea-lucania">Heraclea Lucania</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars link the hill of <b>Policoro</b> with its main settlement.</blockquote><a href="/state/siris">Siris</a></li></ul></section>
